## [International Proposal] Low-Invasive, Infrastructure-Compatible Leukemia Treatment Protocol## ── A Next-Generation Grand Design for Social Implementation via Open-Source (Public Domain) Architecture ──## 1. Analysis of Structural Bottlenecks in Contemporary Medicine
Advanced contemporary leukemia therapies—specifically allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) and chimeric antigen receptor (CAR) T-cell therapy—inherently present two critical existential and operational failures:

   1. The Counter-Productivity of Chemotherapeutic Preconditioning: To facilitate engraftment or expansion of therapeutic cells, current protocols mandate lymphodepleting chemotherapy (cytotoxic agents). This induces severe, prolonged myelosuppression, increasing the incidence of lethal opportunistic infections and long-term secondary malignancies such as therapy-related myelodysplastic syndrome (t-MDS). Consequently, Non-Relapse Mortality (NRM)—where patients succumb to treatment-induced complications despite oncological remission—remains a paramount clinical bottleneck.
   2. Infrastructure Constraints and Prohibitive Costs of Hyper-Individuation: Autologous, patient-specific cell processing requires specialized Clinical Preparation Cleanrooms (CPCs) and ultra-low temperature (liquid nitrogen) logistics. This drives per-patient costs into hundreds of thousands of dollars, structurally restricting therapeutic access to a fraction of affluent populations and highly developed nations.

This Grand Design rejects traditional approaches that necessitate the destruction of both the patient's biological system and existing healthcare infrastructure. Instead, it reconfigures established scientific paradigms to safely eradicate leukemia cells using existing hospital infrastructures without inducing cytotoxic tissue damage.
