## 2. Integrated Therapeutic Formula and Molecular Mechanisms (Scientific Validity)
Utilizing the principles of dynamical systems robustness control from systems biology, this protocol sequentially coordinates two distinct molecular interventions through a single, existing intravenous delivery network:
## Phase I: Preemptive Regulation of the Bone Marrow Microenvironment (Targeted Oral/Injectable Delivery)
To target leukemia stem cells (LSCs)—the primary drivers of relapse that remain dormant in the quiescent G0 phase within the deep bone marrow niche—established, regulatory-approved agents (e.g., G-CSF formulations, Interferon-alpha, or CXCR4 inhibitors) are administered prior to cellular therapy.

* Mechanism: Without damaging the DNA of normal somatic cells, these agents transiently disrupt adhesion molecule configurations, forcing quiescent LSCs into active cell cycle progression and mobilizing them into peripheral circulation, thereby exposing them to targeted elimination.

## Phase II: Allogeneic Stealth Cellular Therapy (Systemic Intravenous Delivery)
This protocol employs off-the-shelf, master-banked allogeneic immune cells (e.g., derived from induced pluripotent stem cells). These cells are gene-edited to knock out Major Histocompatibility Complex (MHC) Class I surface markers (preventing recipient T-cell recognition) while concurrently expressing "don't-eat-me" signals such as CD47 or HLA-E to evade recipient NK-cell mediated clearance.

* Mechanism: Rather than obliterating the patient’s endogenous immune system via chemotherapy, temporary administration of highly selective, non-cytotoxic immunosuppressants (e.g., Tacrolimus) transiently modulates recipient immune reactivity. The stealth therapeutic cells circulate freely without rejection, identifying and eliminating the exposed leukemia cells and LSCs via precise antigen-receptor matching, leaving normal host tissues intact.

