## 3. Physical Compatibility with Existing Clinical Infrastructures (Logistical Bottleneck Resolution)
To resolve the physical barrier of handling live cellular biologies within standard hospital environments, the protocol incorporates validated bio-engineering solutions into standard consumable components, eliminating the need for institutional capital expenditure:

   1. Mitigation of Ultra-Low Temperature Requirements (Cryoprotective Stabilization): By substituting standard cryopreservation media with formulations enriched with protective disaccharides (e.g., high-concentration trehalose matrices), cell viability is maintained in standard pharmacy deep freezers (-80°C) or, for short durations, conventional freezers (-20°C), bypassing the need for liquid nitrogen storage infrastructure.
   2. Prevention of Line Adherence (MPC Polymer Coated Fluidics): To prevent cellular adherence, entrapment, and mechanical lysis against plastic surfaces, the protocol utilizes standard disposable IV administration sets internally coated with biocompatible hydrophilic polymers (e.g., MPC polymer or fluorinated lipid monolayers). This allows clinicians and nursing staff to execute delivery via conventional IV stands and pumps using 100% unaltered protocols.
   3. Streamlined Bedside Processing (Multi-Chamber Container Systems): The cellular payload is distributed in multi-chamber bags segregating the frozen cellular matrix from the thawing/dilution vehicle via a frangible barrier. At the bedside, manual compression of the container ruptures the internal seal, achieving sterile, enclosed, error-free thawing and reconstitution without requiring laminar flow hoods or specialized laboratory technicians.

